#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cw-theme-sync -- make Claude Code's TUI theme follow the browser theme.

WHY THIS EXISTS (the "Reasons" a naive approach fails)
------------------------------------------------------
The ttyd browser terminal already flips xterm.js's *terminal colours*
(background / ANSI palette) to match `prefers-color-scheme` -- see
examples/compose/ttyd/inject-autodark.py. Claude Code's `theme` setting,
when left at "auto", picks light vs dark by probing the terminal's
background colour AT PROCESS STARTUP. So a browser started in dark mode
statically gets a dark Claude TUI. But "auto" is resolved ONCE, at
startup: Claude Code does NOT re-probe the terminal background when it
changes mid-session, so flipping the browser/OS colour scheme while a
session is live leaves the TUI theme stuck. That is the "it doesn't work
dynamically" wall.

THE MECHANISM
-------------
Explicit re-set instead of relying on startup auto-detect:

  browser (prefers-color-scheme change)
    -> POST /theme-report  (clipboard-upload sidecar, examples/compose)
    -> sidecar atomically writes "dark"/"light" to CW_THEME_FILE
       (default /host-clipboard/theme, on the shared volume)
    -> THIS daemon (running inside claude-container) notices the file
       change and injects `/config theme=<dark|light>` into the Claude
       Code tmux pane.

`/config` is a NON-INTERACTIVE slash command (Claude Code >= ~2.1;
`supportsNonInteractive`, `argumentHint:"key=value"`): `/config
theme=dark` sets the theme by key and hot-applies + re-renders WITHOUT a
restart, so the swap is live. (Side effect: it persists an explicit
`theme` value into the settings file, overriding "auto" -- intended: we
are taking over theme control while a browser drives it.)

DESIGN NOTES
------------
  * Idle-gated AND non-cancelling. We only inject when the pane shows an
    idle prompt (the cursor glyph), AND we inject via a plain
    `claude-watch inject` -- which since 2026-08-18 NEVER leads with an
    Escape unless `--escape` is passed. The user's browser theme toggle
    must not cancel the model's in-flight turn, so this script must never
    grow that flag. The idle gate is best-effort (there is a race between
    the check and the inject); the non-cancelling inject is the hard
    guarantee: even if the pane went busy in that window, the `/config
    theme=…` slash command is typed + submitted as a QUEUED message (bare
    Enter from INSERT, no Escape/`dd`), so it lands at the next turn
    boundary instead of interrupting. If the pane is busy at check time the
    desired theme is remembered and applied on a later poll.
  * Debounced / coalescing. We always inject the CURRENT file value, so a
    rapid light->dark->light flip only results in the final theme.
  * Idempotent. We track the last theme we injected and skip no-op
    re-injects.
  * Serialised with EVERY other injector -- inside `claude-watch inject`.
    All injectors drive `tmux send-keys` on the same prompt line, and the
    non-cancelling choreography has no `dd` line-clear, so concurrent
    payloads INTERLEAVE. Observed 2026-08-19: this daemon's
    `/config theme=light` landed spliced into the middle of the word
    "6min" in cw-watcher-health-check's WATCHER DOWN banner --

        unconsumed >6mi/config theme=lightn -- the event watcher is dead

    -- so Claude Code never saw a slash command, the theme never changed,
    and this daemon logged "(verified)" anyway. The lock now lives INSIDE
    `claude-watch inject` (src/inject_lock.rs) rather than in each caller,
    because the daemon's own in-process alerts never shell out to
    `claude-watch inject` at all -- no caller-side convention could cover
    both racing populations. We still take the legacy self-clear flock
    below, but that is only a "stay off the pane during a self-clear"
    courtesy, not the interleave guard.

  * Verified against the EFFECT, not the prompt line. `claude-watch
    inject` reports success when the payload CLEARS from the input line --
    equally true when MANGLED text gets submitted, which is exactly how
    the failure above logged "(verified)". After a successful inject we
    read `theme` back out of Claude Code's settings file (`/config`
    persists an explicit value there) and confirm it matches.

  * Re-assertable on demand. A same-value write to the theme file is a
    no-op by design, so a TUI that drifted out of sync could not be
    corrected without toggling the browser theme twice. `--force` injects
    the current file value regardless of what we think is applied, and
    SIGHUP makes the running daemon re-assert on its next poll.
  * Dependency-free. Pure mtime poll (no inotify), tiny cost; theme
    changes are rare.

Configuration (env, all optional):
  CW_THEME_FILE            theme report file to watch
                           (default /host-clipboard/theme)
  CW_THEME_PANE            tmux pane target (default: auto-resolve via
                           `claude-watch status --json`, else
                           claude-container:0.0)
  CW_THEME_POLL_SECS       mtime poll interval (default 1.5)
  CW_THEME_IDLE_POLL_SECS  how often to re-check idle while a change is
                           pending (default 2.0)
  CW_THEME_LOG             log path (default
                           $XDG_STATE_HOME/claude-watch/theme-sync.log
                           or /var/log/claude-watch/theme-sync.log)
  CW_THEME_LOCK            legacy self-clear coordination lock (default
                           matches self-clear:
                           $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/claude-self-clear.lock or
                           /var/run/claude/claude-self-clear.lock). NOT the
                           inject interleave guard -- that lives inside
                           `claude-watch inject`.
  CW_THEME_SETTINGS_FILE   Claude Code settings file to read `theme` back
                           from for verification (default
                           $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json, else
                           ~/.claude/settings.json)
  CW_THEME_VERIFY_SECS     how long to wait for settings.json to show the
                           requested theme (default 10.0)

Flags:
  --status   print resolved config, current file value, and whether the
             TUI is in sync; exit 0
  --force    inject the CURRENT theme-file value once, regardless of
             whether it changed, then exit. Use to re-assert a TUI that
             has drifted out of sync with the file.

Signals:
  SIGHUP     running daemon forgets what it thinks is applied and
             re-asserts the current theme file value on the next poll.

Runs as a long-lived process under process-compose (see
container/process-compose.yml). Disable with
CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED=true.
"""

import fcntl
import json
import os
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path

# Browser reports one of these; map straight onto Claude Code theme keys.
# We keep it to the two base themes the browser's prefers-color-scheme can
# express; a user who wants the daltonized / ansi variants sets `theme`
# explicitly and would not run this daemon.
VALID = ("dark", "light")

_HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")


def _env(name, default):
    v = os.environ.get(name, "").strip()
    return v if v else default


def _default_log_file():
    state_home = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", "").strip()
    if state_home:
        return str(Path(state_home) / "claude-watch" / "theme-sync.log")
    return "/var/log/claude-watch/theme-sync.log"


def _default_lock_file():
    runtime = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR", "").strip()
    if runtime:
        return str(Path(runtime) / "claude-self-clear.lock")
    return "/var/run/claude/claude-self-clear.lock"


def _default_settings_file():
    """Claude Code's settings file -- where `/config theme=X` persists the
    value we then read back to VERIFY the inject actually took effect."""
    cfg_dir = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "").strip()
    if cfg_dir:
        return str(Path(cfg_dir) / "settings.json")
    return str(Path.home() / ".claude" / "settings.json")


THEME_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_FILE", "/host-clipboard/theme")
POLL_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_POLL_SECS", "1.5"))
IDLE_POLL_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_IDLE_POLL_SECS", "2.0"))
LOG_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_LOG", _default_log_file())
LOCK_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_LOCK", _default_lock_file())
SETTINGS_FILE = _env("CW_THEME_SETTINGS_FILE", _default_settings_file())
VERIFY_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_VERIFY_SECS", "10.0"))

# After this many consecutive verified-failures for the SAME theme value we
# stop retrying it. Without a cap, a genuinely broken /config (Claude Code
# too old, settings file not writable, pane not actually Claude) would have
# us re-injecting into the operator's prompt line every poll, forever.
MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES = 3

# Delay before retry attempt N (seconds). An unbounded retry against a SHARED
# prompt line is a self-inflicted denial of service: on 2026-08-19 the
# every-poll retry put three mangled payloads into the live pane in ~90s,
# which is worse than the silent failure it replaced. Back off hard, then
# give up -- a wrong-coloured TUI is much cheaper than a corrupted input
# stream.
RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS = (30.0, 120.0, 300.0)

# How long the input line must stay empty before we believe the pane is
# genuinely idle rather than being typed into right now.
PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS", "0.8"))

# Set by SIGHUP: forget what we think is applied and re-assert.
_reassert_requested = False

# How long `--force` waits for a busy pane to go idle before giving up. It
# gives up rather than injecting into a busy pane, because forcing the issue
# would mean --escape, and --escape cancels the in-flight turn (Andrew,
# 2026-08-19: "i do NOT want you to inject escape bc that interrupts").
FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS = float(_env("CW_THEME_FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS", "60.0"))


def _on_sighup(_signum, _frame):
    """SIGHUP => re-assert the current theme on the next poll.

    Gives a running daemon the same escape hatch `--force` gives a one-shot
    caller, without a second process racing it onto the pane.
    """
    global _reassert_requested
    _reassert_requested = True


def log(msg):
    line = f"[{datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')}] {msg}"
    try:
        with open(LOG_FILE, "a") as f:
            f.write(line + "\n")
    except Exception:
        pass
    try:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
    except Exception:
        pass


def run(cmd, timeout=10):
    """Run a subprocess; return (stdout, rc). Never raises."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        return r.stdout.strip(), r.returncode
    except Exception:
        return "", -1


def resolve_pane():
    """Resolve the Claude Code tmux pane.

    Command-name INDEPENDENT: the native installer runs claude as a
    version-string command, so we never match on command name. Prefer
    `claude-watch status --json` (authoritative), then the env override,
    then the conventional container pane. `claude-watch inject` does its
    own resolution too, but we need a concrete pane for the idle check.
    """
    env_pane = os.environ.get("CW_THEME_PANE", "").strip()
    if env_pane:
        return env_pane
    try:
        out, _ = run(["claude-watch", "status", "--json"])
        data = json.loads(out)
        pane = data.get("pane") or data.get("pane_id")
        if pane:
            return pane
    except Exception:
        pass
    return "claude-container:0.0"


def _prompt_line(pane_text):
    """Text currently sitting on the `❯` input line, or None if no prompt
    is rendered. '' means the input line is empty."""
    for line in reversed(pane_text.split("\n")):
        if "❯" in line:
            return line.split("❯", 1)[1].strip()
    return None


def is_idle(pane):
    """True only when it is SAFE to type into the pane.

    The original check -- "is the `❯` glyph anywhere in the last 15 lines"
    -- was far too weak. Claude Code renders that prompt box permanently,
    including while a turn is generating, so it reported idle for a pane that
    was mid-composition. On 2026-08-19 that let three theme injects land in an
    actively-generating pane.

    Four gates now, each closing a distinct way the old one was wrong:

      1. A prompt must actually be rendered (`❯` present).
      2. No in-flight turn -- Claude Code shows "esc to interrupt" in the
         status bar for the whole duration of one.
      3. THE INPUT LINE MUST BE EMPTY. This is the direct guard against the
         bug: the non-cancelling inject has no `dd` line-clear, so anything
         already on that line gets our payload glued onto it. If someone (or
         something) has half-typed input sitting there, typing is exactly the
         wrong move.
      4. The input line must still be empty a moment later, so we don't race
         a human who just started typing.

    Deliberately NOT a full-pane stability check: the pane carries a ticking
    token counter and agent elapsed-time line, so it is never byte-stable and
    such a check would report "never idle".
    """
    out, rc = run(["tmux", "capture-pane", "-t", pane, "-p"])
    if rc != 0 or not out:
        return False
    if "esc to interrupt" in out.lower():
        return False                      # a turn is generating
    first = _prompt_line(out)
    if first is None or first != "":
        return False                      # no prompt, or input already there
    time.sleep(PROMPT_RECHECK_SECS)
    out2, rc2 = run(["tmux", "capture-pane", "-t", pane, "-p"])
    if rc2 != 0 or not out2:
        return False
    if "esc to interrupt" in out2.lower():
        return False
    return _prompt_line(out2) == ""


def read_theme():
    """Read + validate the reported theme; return 'dark'/'light' or None."""
    try:
        val = Path(THEME_FILE).read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip().lower()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        log(f"read error: {e}")
        return None
    if val in VALID:
        return val
    if val:
        log(f"ignoring unrecognised theme value {val!r} (want one of {VALID})")
    return None


def read_settings_theme():
    """Read the CURRENTLY APPLIED theme out of Claude Code's settings file.

    This is the ground truth for verification. `/config theme=X` hot-applies
    AND persists an explicit `theme` key here, so the file agreeing with what
    we asked for is real evidence the slash command was parsed and executed --
    unlike "the payload left the prompt line", which is equally true when
    mangled text gets submitted.

    Returns 'dark'/'light', the raw string for any other value (e.g. 'auto',
    a daltonized variant a human set by hand), or None if the file is
    missing / unreadable / not JSON.
    """
    try:
        raw = Path(SETTINGS_FILE).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        log(f"settings read error: {e}")
        return None
    try:
        val = json.loads(raw).get("theme")
    except Exception as e:
        # A concurrent write can catch us mid-rewrite; the caller polls, so a
        # transient parse failure just costs one attempt.
        log(f"settings parse error: {e}")
        return None
    if isinstance(val, str):
        return val.strip().lower()
    return None


def verify_applied(theme):
    """Poll the settings file until it reports `theme`, up to VERIFY_SECS.

    Returns (ok, detail):
      (True,  'settings.json theme=<theme>')  -- confirmed applied.
      (False, 'settings.json theme=<other>')  -- REAL failure; /config did
                                                 not take (this is the
                                                 2026-08-19 mangled-inject
                                                 case).
      (True,  'UNVERIFIABLE: ...')            -- settings file unreadable.
                                                 We cannot prove success OR
                                                 failure, so we do NOT retry
                                                 (retrying types into the
                                                 operator's prompt line every
                                                 poll, forever) but we also
                                                 never claim "verified".
    """
    deadline = time.monotonic() + VERIFY_SECS
    seen = None
    while True:
        seen = read_settings_theme()
        if seen == theme:
            return True, f"settings.json theme={theme}"
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            break
        time.sleep(0.5)
    if seen is None:
        return True, f"UNVERIFIABLE: cannot read theme from {SETTINGS_FILE}"
    return False, f"settings.json theme={seen!r}, wanted {theme!r}"


def inject_theme(pane, theme):
    """Inject `/config theme=<theme>` as a slash command. Returns True on
    a verified submit.

    Serialised with self-clear via the shared non-blocking flock: if
    self-clear (or a prior inject) holds it, skip and retry next cycle.
    """
    try:
        lock_fd = os.open(LOCK_FILE, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o644)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"lock open failed ({e}); injecting without send-keys lock")
        lock_fd = None
    if lock_fd is not None:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except (OSError, BlockingIOError):
            log("send-keys lock contended (self-clear active?); retry next cycle")
            os.close(lock_fd)
            return False
    try:
        cmd = [
            "claude-watch", "inject",
            "--pane", pane,
            "--slash-command",
            # NON-CANCELLING, and deliberately NO `--escape`: inject never
            # leads with an Escape unless asked to (2026-08-18), so a theme
            # toggle can never cancel the model's in-flight turn. Types +
            # submits the slash command as a queued message (bare Enter from
            # INSERT). This is the hard guarantee behind the idle-gate above:
            # even if the pane became busy in the race window between the idle
            # check and this inject, the theme change is queued behind the
            # active turn, not interrupted. Do not add `--escape` here.
            "--submit", f"/config theme={theme}",
        ]
        out, rc = run(cmd, timeout=30)
        if rc == 0:
            # rc==0 only means the payload LEFT THE PROMPT LINE. It does not
            # mean Claude Code parsed a slash command -- a mangled payload
            # submits and clears just the same. Confirm the EFFECT instead.
            ok, detail = verify_applied(theme)
            if ok:
                log(f"injected /config theme={theme} ({detail})")
                return True
            log(
                f"inject /config theme={theme}: submit landed but theme did NOT "
                f"change ({detail}) -- payload likely mangled on the prompt line; retry"
            )
            return False
        if rc == 3:
            log(f"inject /config theme={theme}: submit NOT verified (rc=3); retry")
        else:
            log(f"inject /config theme={theme} failed rc={rc} out={out!r}; retry")
        return False
    finally:
        if lock_fd is not None:
            try:
                os.close(lock_fd)
            except Exception:
                pass


def print_status():
    pane = resolve_pane()
    want = read_theme()
    have = read_settings_theme()
    print(f"theme_file    : {THEME_FILE}")
    print(f"theme_value   : {want!r}")
    print(f"settings_file : {SETTINGS_FILE}")
    print(f"settings_theme: {have!r}")
    if want is None or have is None:
        sync = "unknown"
    elif want == have:
        sync = "in-sync"
    else:
        sync = "OUT OF SYNC (run --force to re-assert)"
    print(f"in_sync       : {sync}")
    print(f"pane          : {pane}")
    print(f"pane_idle     : {is_idle(pane)}")
    print(f"lock_file     : {LOCK_FILE}")
    print(f"log_file      : {LOG_FILE}")
    return 0


def force_once():
    """Re-assert the CURRENT theme-file value, ignoring what we think is
    already applied.

    Exists because the daemon is deliberately idempotent: it skips a re-inject
    when the file value equals the last value it applied, and a same-value
    write to the theme file is a no-op. That is correct for the normal path
    (the frontend re-POSTs on every page load) but it means a TUI that drifted
    out of sync -- exactly what the 2026-08-19 mangled inject left behind --
    could not be corrected without toggling the browser theme twice.

    Waits for the pane to go idle, bounded, rather than injecting into a busy
    pane. Still NON-cancelling: no --escape anywhere.
    """
    theme = read_theme()
    if theme is None:
        log(f"--force: no usable theme value in {THEME_FILE}; nothing to do")
        return 1
    pane = resolve_pane()
    deadline = time.monotonic() + FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS
    while not is_idle(pane):
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            log(
                f"--force: pane {pane} still busy after {FORCE_IDLE_WAIT_SECS}s; "
                f"NOT injecting (would have to seize the turn to do so)"
            )
            return 2
        time.sleep(IDLE_POLL_SECS)
    log(f"--force: re-asserting theme={theme} on pane {pane}")
    return 0 if inject_theme(pane, theme) else 3


def main():
    global _reassert_requested
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--status" in argv:
        return print_status()
    force = "--force" in argv

    try:
        Path(LOG_FILE).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        pass

    if _env("CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED", "false").lower() in (
        "1", "true", "yes",
    ):
        log("CLAUDE_CONTAINER_THEME_SYNC_DISABLED set; exiting 0 (disabled)")
        return 0

    if force:
        return force_once()

    # SIGHUP => re-assert on the next poll. Registered only for the daemon
    # path; the one-shot paths above have already returned.
    try:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGHUP, _on_sighup)
    except (ValueError, OSError):
        pass

    log(
        f"START: watching {THEME_FILE} (poll {POLL_SECS}s), "
        f"verify={SETTINGS_FILE}, legacy lock={LOCK_FILE}"
    )

    applied = None          # last theme we VERIFIED as applied
    desired = None          # last theme read from the file (may await idle)
    last_mtime = None
    verify_failures = 0     # consecutive failed verifications of `desired`
    retry_not_before = 0.0  # monotonic deadline enforcing RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS

    while True:
        # SIGHUP asks us to re-assert even though the file value has not
        # changed.
        if _reassert_requested:
            _reassert_requested = False
            log("SIGHUP: re-asserting current theme (forgetting applied state)")
            applied = None
            verify_failures = 0
            retry_not_before = 0.0
            last_mtime = None

        try:
            mtime = os.path.getmtime(THEME_FILE)
        except OSError:
            mtime = None
        if mtime != last_mtime:
            last_mtime = mtime
            val = read_theme()
            if val and val != desired:
                log(f"theme reported: {val}")
                desired = val
                verify_failures = 0
                retry_not_before = 0.0

        if desired and desired != applied:
            # Backoff gate: after a failed attempt we wait out
            # RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS before touching the pane again.
            if time.monotonic() < retry_not_before:
                time.sleep(min(IDLE_POLL_SECS, POLL_SECS))
                continue

            pane = resolve_pane()
            if is_idle(pane):
                if inject_theme(pane, desired):
                    applied = desired
                    verify_failures = 0
                    retry_not_before = 0.0
                else:
                    verify_failures += 1
                    if verify_failures >= MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES:
                        # Stop hammering the operator's prompt line. Mark it
                        # applied so we go quiet until the theme file changes
                        # again (or someone sends SIGHUP / runs --force), and
                        # say so LOUDLY -- this is the state the old code hid
                        # behind a bogus "(verified)".
                        log(
                            f"GIVING UP on theme={desired} after "
                            f"{verify_failures} failed verifications; the TUI is "
                            f"OUT OF SYNC with {THEME_FILE}. Investigate, then "
                            f"re-assert with `cw-theme-sync --force` or SIGHUP."
                        )
                        applied = desired
                        verify_failures = 0
                        retry_not_before = 0.0
                    else:
                        backoff = RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS[
                            min(verify_failures - 1, len(RETRY_BACKOFF_SECS) - 1)
                        ]
                        retry_not_before = time.monotonic() + backoff
                        log(
                            f"backing off {backoff:.0f}s before retry "
                            f"{verify_failures + 1}/{MAX_VERIFY_FAILURES} of "
                            f"theme={desired}"
                        )
                time.sleep(POLL_SECS)
                continue
            time.sleep(min(IDLE_POLL_SECS, POLL_SECS))
            continue

        time.sleep(POLL_SECS)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(0)
